Cell[TextData[{
 "The computed ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["F", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["Q", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "60afd8f3-6eb1-6601-58a1-f963b4a38da5"],
 " equals both closed forms for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["n", "TI"], "=", "2"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "09e7e7f7-732e-ffbe-1b5e-fa5f1101c7fe"],
 ", and each marker sits at ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], "/", "4", 
   StyleBox["e", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["\[Gamma]", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4376077c-6236-9e59-74ae-f0db4683ac93"],
 ". For short runs the GHZ probe gathers information faster, but the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], "2"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "135e3166-834c-8162-79ff-81ed861dfde0"],
 " in its ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["F", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["Q", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6c992dbf-0114-ba98-4347-390350d5f416"],
 " comes with a decay ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "67fc7fa9-9a72-e57a-0867-f6bb8cf632f2"],
 " times faster, so its best run is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "673d786c-f5c9-7d02-41fc-8b78ab2e5ad9"],
 " times shorter and reaches exactly the same rate as the product probe: \
under independent dephasing the Heisenberg gain cancels at the optimum."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1111238958,ExpressionUUID->"1139e14d-25f3-c429-39cd-5e9a258db084"],

Cell["\[NonBreakingSpace]", "SlideShowNavigationBar",
 CellTags->"SlideShowHeader",
 CellID->1233443122,ExpressionUUID->"5a7b4289-ff51-4df7-49e6-58e1db0b0734"],

Cell["Exact quantum dynamics", "Section",
 CellID->1202661899,ExpressionUUID->"494c9dd2-32f4-212a-7f86-02116bc415d4"],

Cell["\<\
The examples in this section are closed systems solved exactly. A qubit is \
driven by a rotating field, swept through an avoided crossing (Landau-Zener), \
or flipped by a Gaussian pulse; two spins exchange an excitation; a coherent \
state circles in a harmonic well; a speed limit bounds how far any drive can \
move a state; spins point along a direction and pass through a sequence of \
Stern-Gerlach magnets; and a chirped rotation is inverted for the Hamiltonian \
that drives it. Spectra come next: an NV center in a tilted field, a \
Heisenberg ring split by its symmetries, a variational bound on a \
ground-state energy, Fermi's golden rule emerging from a level coupled to a \
band, and the populations an atom settles at in a thermal cavity. QF builds \
the Hamiltonians and states, and WL solves the Schr\[ODoubleDot]dinger \
equation for the full propagator, often with every parameter left symbolic, \
so the answer is a formula that the figure then draws.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1496683409,ExpressionUUID->"268ef114-6bb3-a34f-586c-b092c5c45daf"],

Cell["\[NonBreakingSpace]", "SlideShowNavigationBar",
